    "notes": "Four commercially available CAP devices (two medical, two cosmetic), based on dielectric barrier discharge (DBD) concepts (volume and surface discharge) were investigated in a controlled in vivo study on ten healthy volunteers. Skin barrier related parameters were assessed using transepidermal water loss (TEWL) and electrical impedance at 1 kHz. Skin hydration and microcirculation were employed to capture additional plasma-induced effects. From: C. Hellwig et al. 2026 Skin Research and Technology [Under Review]",
